WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.900
Middleware order is part of ASP.NET Core security behavior.

2
00:00:05.000 --> 00:00:14.360
CORS must be placed intentionally in relation to routing, authentication, authorization, static files, response caching, and endpoint mapping.

3
00:00:14.460 --> 00:00:23.340
If the policy runs too early, too late, or on the wrong branch of the pipeline, it may appear not to work or may apply differently than intended.

4
00:00:23.620 --> 00:00:29.300
Routing and endpoint metadata are especially important when CORS decisions are endpoint-specific.

5
00:00:29.400 --> 00:00:38.280
The application needs enough routing context to know which endpoint is being evaluated, and the CORS middleware needs to run before the endpoint response is finalized.

6
00:00:38.380 --> 00:00:47.740
When module, route, and policy intent are aligned, reviewers can understand why a particular endpoint allows a particular browser origin.

7
00:00:48.020 --> 00:00:53.700
Static files, response caching, gateways, and proxies can change expectations.

8
00:00:53.800 --> 00:00:57.800
A cached response may not include the headers the browser expects.

9
00:00:57.900 --> 00:01:03.420
A static file path may not pass through the same policy as API endpoints.

10
00:01:03.520 --> 00:01:06.960
A gateway may add, remove, or duplicate headers.

11
00:01:07.060 --> 00:01:10.820
A proxy may answer OPTIONS before the application sees the request.

12
00:01:10.920 --> 00:01:16.360
These are configuration issues, not reasons to broaden application policy without review.

13
00:01:16.640 --> 00:01:19.440
Endpoint scope matters as much as middleware order.

14
00:01:19.540 --> 00:01:33.540
A policy intended for a public browser client should not automatically cover administrative endpoints, operational endpoints, internal APIs, or routes that were never meant for cross-origin browser use.

15
00:01:33.640 --> 00:01:38.680
Grouping endpoints and assigning policies intentionally makes the application easier to reason about.

16
00:01:38.960 --> 00:01:45.200
CORS should not become an accidental global permission added only to make browser errors disappear.

17
00:01:45.300 --> 00:01:53.940
A browser error is a signal to check origin, method, headers, credentials, middleware placement, endpoint scope, and infrastructure behavior.

18
00:01:54.040 --> 00:01:59.560
The fix should match the business requirement rather than create broad permission for unknown future clients.
